{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791623157,"nonce":"5BB/SPz4Zw2Ns0qwPnu/KKQgFZ8=","title":"Pipeline passes rebuild sets and copy instruction lists they only read","message":"A static-hot body waits for the whole pass pipeline before its first call (28fefa0). Sampling 150 repeated start-ups of method_call (Mac, llvm-backend build), the pipeline under run_interp_safe_opts_with splits inline 364 samples, drop_insert 326, cse 303, phi_prune 220, const_fold 196, licm 175, sign_fold 118, error_flag 115, aggregate_scalarize 113. Avoidable work inside them:\n\n- phi_prune::live_phis put every operand of every instruction into a HashSet (insert and rehash were 81 of its 287 samples) and only then kept the phi results; its hand-rolled operand walk fell back to Debug-formatting unknown instructions.\n- cse interned each instruction's result type through HashMap\u003cHirType, u32\u003e, hashing and comparing the type tree per instruction (43 of 426 samples).\n- inline_in_function cloned each block's instruction list to scan it for calls, every iteration of every round; const_fold::fold_one_pass cloned and rebuilt every block's list even when nothing folded.\n- known_bits, error_flag, branch_fold, affine_loop and drop_insert hashed HirId keys with SipHash; drop_insert's id sets, licm and load_cse used FNV.\n\nFix: live_phis seeds from HirInstruction/HirTerminator::for_each_operand straight into the live set; cse numbers scalar types and pointers to scalars without a lookup; the inliner borrows the block it scans; fold_one_pass folds with retain in place; those maps are Fx (IdHashMap/IdHashSet).\n\nStart-up instructions (llvm-backend build, PROFILE_STARTUP=10, median of 3, Mac): Lua 17 benchmarks 24.39G -\u003e 23.24G (-4.7%; method_call -7.5%, op_overload -6.9%, records -5.8%, tokenize -5.6%, pipeline -5.4%, mandelbrot -4.3%, nbody -3.4%; fib +0.3%); Python 8 kernels -0.4 to -1.2% each. Optimised bodies identical after id masking on all 17 Lua benchmarks and 6 Python kernels (249 bodies) with ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1; outputs unchanged.","files":null}]}